---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<Layout title="About — HDX Core Data">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="content">
      <h1>HDX Core Data</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        An interactive explorer for humanitarian crisis data published by OCHA through the
        <a href="https://hdx-hapi.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Humanitarian API (HAPI)</a>.
        All data loads directly in your browser from cloud-native Parquet files — no account, no download, no server.
      </p>

      <h2>Visualizations</h2>

      <div class="viz-grid">
        <a class="viz-card" href={`${base}`}>
          <h3>Explorer</h3>
          <p>
            A Gapminder-style bubble chart that plots countries and sub-national regions on any two
            humanitarian indicators simultaneously. Each bubble represents a country or admin region;
            size encodes a third variable. Step through years to watch crises evolve, or drill from
            a country down to its provinces and districts.
          </p>
          <p class="axes-note">
            Available axes: conflict fatalities, IPC food crisis (Phase 3+), poverty headcount,
            multidimensional poverty (MPI), national risk score, humanitarian funding gap, people in
            humanitarian need, refugees hosted, rainfall anomaly, IDP population, and total population.
          </p>
        </a>

        <a class="viz-card" href={`${base}food-security`}>
          <h3>Food Security</h3>
          <p>
            A ranked bar chart of IPC (Integrated Food Security Phase Classification) phase
            distributions across all countries with data. Each bar shows what share of the assessed
            population falls in each phase — from Phase 1 (Minimal) through Phase 5 (Famine).
          </p>
          <p>
            Use the year slider to compare across seasons, and sort by Phase 5, Phase 4+, or Phase 3+
            to surface the most acute crises.
          </p>
        </a>

        <a class="viz-card" href={`${base}timeline`}>
          <h3>Crisis Timeline</h3>
          <p>
            A country-level time series showing four crisis indicators in parallel panels: conflict
            fatalities (ACLED), internally displaced persons, IPC Phase 3+ population share, and
            humanitarian funding gap. Useful for understanding how different dimensions of a crisis
            have evolved together over time.
          </p>
          <p>Filtered to countries with an active Humanitarian Response Plan (HRP) or in the Global Humanitarian Overview.</p>
        </a>

        <a class="viz-card" href={`${base}food-prices`}>
          <h3>Food Prices</h3>
          <p>
            Tracks WFP food price series for a selected country and commodity category. Prices can
            be shown as raw values or normalized to a baseline, making it easier to compare
            volatility across markets and commodities.
          </p>
        </a>

        <a class="viz-card" href={`${base}refugees`}>
          <h3>Refugee Flows</h3>
          <p>
            A chord diagram mapping refugee and asylum seeker movements between origin and asylum
            countries. The width of each arc is proportional to the number of people. Useful for
            seeing which countries are major sources or hosts of forced displacement.
          </p>
        </a>

        <a class="viz-card" href={`${base}availability`}>
          <h3>Data Availability</h3>
          <p>
            A catalog of the underlying HAPI datasets, showing which countries have data for each
            indicator. Use this to understand gaps in coverage before drawing conclusions from the
            other views.
          </p>
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2>Data source</h2>
      <p>
        All datasets come from <a href="https://hdx-hapi.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OCHA HAPI</a>
        and are hosted as Parquet files on <a href="https://source.coop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source Cooperative</a>.
        The browser reads them directly using DuckDB-WASM — no data ever passes through a server.
      </p>
      <p>
        Underlying sources include ACLED (conflict), IPC (food security), WFP (food prices),
        UNHCR (refugees and IDPs), OCHA FTS (funding), and national statistics (population and poverty).
      </p>

      <h2>Design principle</h2>
      <p>
        Humanitarian data without context can mislead. Raw counts tell you a country has a large
        number of displaced people; rates tell you whether that represents 2% or 40% of its
        population. Wherever datasets provide pre-calculated rates or fractions, HDX Core Data uses
        them. When showing counts, it contextualizes them against population.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page {
    height: 100%;
    overflow-y: auto;
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .content {
    max-width: 860px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 24px 80px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 28px;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 40px 0 14px;
    color: var(--text);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--text-sep);
    padding-bottom: 6px;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  a {
    color: #f46d43;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .viz-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(360px, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  .viz-card {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--text-sep);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px 22px;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s, background 0.15s;
  }
  .viz-card:hover {
    border-color: #f46d43;
    background: var(--hover-bg);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .viz-card h3 {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 10px;
  }

  .viz-card p {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
  }
  .viz-card p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .axes-note {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--text-sep);
  }
</style>
